.TH write "" "Command"
.SH NAME
write \- conduct interactive conversation with another user
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwrite \fIuser \fB[\fItty\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
write -- conduct interactive conversation with another user
Usage:	write user [ tty ]
.HE
.B write
opens a two-way conversation with another logged-in user.
It looks up
.I user
in
.BR /etc/utmp ,
using
.I tty
to pick a particular login if that
.I user
is logged in on more than one terminal, and opens the corresponding
terminal for writing.
It then prints
.B "User user has been notified ..."
on the caller's own terminal, and copies each character typed at its
standard input to the recipient's terminal until end of file (the
terminal's end-of-file character, if the input is a terminal) is
reached.
.PP
If the recipient's terminal denies writes (its execute permission bit
for "other" is off) and the caller is the superuser,
.B write
asks
.B "User `user' is denying messages.  Override?"
and proceeds only if the answer is
.BR y ;
any other caller is simply refused.
.PP
While the conversation is open,
.B write
puts its own terminal into cbreak mode so it can watch for lines
beginning with
.BR ! .
Such a line is not sent to the recipient; instead, the remainder of the
line is read as a command and run with
.BR system() ,
with its output appearing locally, after which the conversation
resumes.
If both terminals are in "crt" mode (see
.BR stty ),
.B write
also echoes backspace characters specially so that character erase
looks right at both ends.
.PP
The conversation ends when the sender reaches end of file (interrupt,
hangup, and quit are also caught and treated the same way); both
terminals are restored to their previous modes, and
.B write
sends the line
.B EOT
to the recipient's terminal before exiting.
.B write
runs setuid to root so that it can test, and when permitted override,
the destination terminal's write-permission bit; it drops this
privilege with
.B setuid(getuid())
immediately after opening the destination terminal.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/etc/utmp\fR	list of logged-in users
\fB/dev/*\fR	terminal special files
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR who ,
.BR wall ,
.BR msg ,
.B mesg
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B write
prints a diagnostic and exits with status 1 if
.I user
is not logged in, is not logged in on the named
.IR tty ,
the destination terminal cannot be written to, or the recipient
refuses the connection; a warning only (without exiting) is printed
if the caller's own terminal cannot be replied to.
.SH NOTES
.B write
prints
.B "Warning: One or both ttys is not a screen-oriented device. ..."
if the local terminal is not in "crt" mode and the standard input is a
terminal, since character-erase (backspace) handling between the two
ends may then not look right.
